# openrc-init is selected via the init= kernel param in auto/config;
# devuan's default init (sysvinit) stays untouched.
openrc

# isolinux.bin moved into its own package when syslinux split in 2014;
# this live-build's package check doesn't know that, so pull it in by hand.
isolinux

# dhcpcd probes every interface it finds and doesn't need to know real
# interface names ahead of time - needed for tarnod's mistral calls to
# reach the internet at all. Handles wifi the same way once iwd (below)
# has associated an interface - nothing extra to wire up for that.
dhcpcd5

# WiFi: iwd (iNet wireless daemon) instead of wpa_supplicant + a
# separate GUI/CLI tool - it's a single self-contained daemon with its
# own simple `iwctl` client, no supplicant.conf to hand-edit, and it
# doesn't fight dhcpcd for IP configuration (iwd only does L2
# association, dhcpcd still does DHCP for every interface it finds,
# wireless included - see 0500-iwd-enable.chroot). tarno-settings'
# Network tab drives it via iwctl.
#
# The actual chip firmware (not code, just binary blobs) for real WiFi
# hardware lives in the non-free-firmware archive area (enabled in
# auto/config) - broad coverage on purpose since this has to work on
# whatever laptop it's actually tested on, not just one chipset:
#   firmware-iwlwifi    - Intel (by far the most common in laptops)
#   firmware-realtek    - Realtek (common in cheaper/newer hardware)
#   firmware-atheros    - Qualcomm Atheros (ath10k/ath11k need this;
#                         ath9k is fully open and doesn't, but including
#                         it costs nothing and covers the mixed case)
#   firmware-brcm80211  - Broadcom (common in older Macbooks and some
#                         Chromebook-derived hardware)
#   firmware-misc-nonfree - grab-bag for everything else non-free that
#                           doesn't fit the above (some older/oddball
#                           wifi and bluetooth combo chips)
iwd
firmware-iwlwifi
firmware-realtek
firmware-atheros
firmware-brcm80211
firmware-misc-nonfree

# isohybrid moved into its own package too (same 2014 syslinux split as
# isolinux above). lb_binary_iso's own check for it only looks for the
# `syslinux` package (Check_package chroot/usr/bin/isohybrid syslinux),
# which doesn't ship isohybrid anymore either - same class of stale
# check, so pull it in by hand like isolinux.
syslinux-utils

# desktop: labwc (minimal wlroots/openbox-alike compositor with a real
# root-menu, see config/includes.chroot/etc/xdg/labwc/) + seatd (seat
# management without systemd-logind) + foot (minimal wayland terminal,
# also the menu's Terminal entry) + swaybg (labwc, like Openbox, paints
# no background of its own - without a wallpaper client the screen is
# just black, indistinguishable from "did this even start", confirmed
# on a real boot) + waybar (taskbar - window list via its wlr/taskbar
# module, which needs no compositor-specific support beyond
# wlr-foreign-toplevel-management, which labwc implements - plus a
# clock and a Tarno Settings launcher button, config in
# config/includes.chroot/etc/xdg/waybar/) + wlr-randr (labwc has no
# way to force a specific output mode itself - its own manual points
# at wlr-randr/kanshi for that - needed by
# etc/xdg/labwc/autostart's preferred-mode fix, see its comment for
# the real bug this addresses: a real boot rendered the whole desktop
# squashed into a square aspect ratio).
labwc
seatd
foot
swaybg
waybar
wlr-randr

# settings panel (config/includes.chroot/usr/bin/tarno-settings)
python3
python3-pyside6.qtcore
python3-pyside6.qtgui
python3-pyside6.qtwidgets

# tarno-disk-install (config/includes.chroot/usr/bin/tarno-disk-install,
# menu entry in etc/xdg/labwc/menu.xml) needs all of these to actually
# do anything - partition/format/copy/bootloader. Previously not shipped
# in the image at all, so the installer only ever existed as source you'd
# have to build yourself; there was no way to install to disk from a
# running live session despite the README claiming otherwise.
#
# grub-pc-bin ships the i386-pc target modules for BIOS/legacy
# grub-install (confirmed needed: a real install hung at "boot from
# disk" because only the EFI target was ever installed, on a BIOS-mode
# VM - see cmd/tarno-disk-install's biosBoot()). Deliberately
# grub-pc-bin, not grub-pc - the latter's postinst asks (via debconf)
# which disk to install to, which has no good answer inside a live
# image's own build chroot.
parted
dosfstools
e2fsprogs
rsync
grub-efi-amd64
grub-pc-bin

# The `user` account itself is now created at build time
# (hooks/0175-user-account.chroot), not by live-config's own
# 0030-user-setup runtime component - so the `user-setup` package that
# component needs is no longer pulled in at all; it would just sit
# there unused (its component self-skips once the account already
# exists, same as ours creating it early). `sudo` is still needed
# though, for the actual `sudo` binary every `sudo -n ...` call in this
# image shells out to, and for live-config's own 0040-sudo component
# (which - independent of user-setup - adds `user` to the `sudo` group
# if the `sudo` package is present; harmless/redundant now that
# etc/sudoers.d/tarno-user grants NOPASSWD directly by username rather
# than through that group, but no reason to fight it).
sudo

# core desktop apps - up to now this was labwc's right-click menu,
# tarno-settings, and a bare terminal, nothing to actually browse
# files, get online, or edit a document with.
#   pcmanfm-qt - file manager (LXQt's, Qt-based to match tarno-settings)
#   firefox-esr - web browser (nothing at all was installed before)
#   geany - text editor with syntax highlighting, still lightweight
#   fuzzel - app launcher (same wlroots-ecosystem author as foot),
#     bound to Super+Space in rc.xml, reads /usr/share/applications/*
#     .desktop files automatically - no extra config needed for that
#   wl-clipboard, grim, slurp - clipboard and screenshot CLI tools;
#     baseline utilities every other wlroots desktop ships, missing
#     here entirely until now
pcmanfm-qt
firefox-esr
geany
fuzzel
wl-clipboard
grim
slurp

# pcmanfm-qt's own stock defaults reference a terminal and archiver we
# don't have (qterminal, lxqt-archiver - neither is in its own Depends/
# Recommends), confirmed real on a boot ("Operation not supported" /
# "No such file or directory" doing basic file operations - see
# etc/skel/.config/pcmanfm-qt/lxqt/settings.conf for the rest of that
# fix). xarchiver is the actual archive manager now referenced there.
xarchiver

# ~/Desktop, ~/Downloads etc. never existed - nothing in this image was
# creating them (see etc/profile.d/tarno-desktop.sh).
xdg-user-dirs

# papirus-icon-theme is one of several OR-alternatives in pcmanfm-qt's
# own Recommends (papirus | breeze | faenza | oxygen) - apt's resolver
# could just as easily pick any of the others. Both pcmanfm-qt's
# settings.conf and tarno-store explicitly reference "Papirus" by
# name, so pull it in for real instead of leaving that to chance.
papirus-icon-theme

# app installer method: synaptic is a real, complete graphical apt
# frontend (search/browse/install/remove across every package in
# sources.list, not just a curated list) - menu entry in
# etc/xdg/labwc/menu.xml. Complements tarno-store (config/includes.
# chroot/usr/bin/tarno-store), a small curated catalog of ~20 apps
# across the categories a usable desktop needs, installed/removed with
# a couple of clicks via plain apt-get underneath.
synaptic

# tarno-store's Flathub tab shells out to `flatpak search`/`install`/
# `uninstall --user` directly - this is the actual `flatpak` CLI/
# runtime itself, not just a library. The flathub remote is added at
# login instead of at build time (see etc/profile.d/tarno-desktop.sh)
# since --user remotes are per-account, not global. --user (not
# --system) deliberately avoids needing polkit/root for installs -
# this image has no desktop polkit agent, same single-user reasoning
# as tarnod's world-writable socket and the NOPASSWD sudoers drop-in.
flatpak
